Why this does not make the whole skill ShareAlike
-------------------------------------------------

CC BY-SA 4.0 attaches its ShareAlike condition to "Adapted Material", which
section 1(a) defines as material "derived from or based upon the Licensed
Material and in which the Licensed Material is translated, altered, arranged,
transformed, or otherwise modified in a manner requiring permission".

The excerpts here are unmodified, and the scripts, assets and documents are
independent works that sit beside them rather than being derived from them. On
that reading the ShareAlike condition covers the excerpts and not the rest.

This is stated as the basis for how the files are marked, not as legal advice.
If you redistribute this skill, keep this NOTICE and the per-file headers. If
you need a distribution with no CC BY-SA material in it at all, delete
tests/fixtures/clean/ — you lose the false-positive tests that depend on it and
nothing else.

Verified rather than assumed, on 2026-09-17: with the directory removed, the
suite runs 148 tests and reports FAILED (failures=3), all three in
test_false_positives.py — test_corpus_contract,
test_sources_and_bands_are_spread and test_hard_rules_stay_within_budget. It
does not report "NO TESTS RAN", and it does not pass over an empty corpus,
which would be the dangerous outcome: a false-positive budget that silently
holds because nothing was measured.


Measured values derived from BAREC
----------------------------------

The sentence-length bands and verbal-ratio figures in assets/registers.json and
assets/profiles.json were measured by this project over BAREC. They are
measurements, and each one publishes the corpus, the column, the counts and the
method needed to recompute it (see references/registers.md and
references/sources.md). BAREC is credited there as the corpus they were
measured over.


Sources consulted but NOT redistributed here
--------------------------------------------

The rules cite the Netflix Arabic Timed Text Style Guide, the World Bank
Translation Style Guide, decisions of Majmaʿ al-Lugha al-ʿArabiyya (Cairo) and
of the Arabic Language Academy of Damascus, Aḥmad Zakī Bāšā (1912) and ʿAbd
al-Salām Muḥammad Hārūn. None of those documents are included in this
distribution. references/sources.md carries the chain to each one so any
finding can be re-verified at its source.
